23:27:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It’s understandable that your parents are concerned—any job with constant exposure to alcohol can raise questions about drinking habits. That said, being a bartender doesn’t automatically lead to unhealthy drinking. Many people in the industry are mindful professionals who set boundaries, and for some, the environment actually reduces the temptation because they see the effects of overconsumption on customers. That said, there are real risks. The social culture can normalize heavy drinking, late-night shift patterns can make it easy to fall into a routine of drinking after work, and easy access can blur lines. Research does show that hospitality workers have higher rates of alcohol consumption and sometimes alcohol use disorders compared to the general population, though this doesn’t mean every bartender develops a problem. So, your parents aren’t wrong to be vigilant, but constant worry without evidence can strain your relationship with your sister. A better approach might be to keep communication open, show interest in her new job, and watch for any changes like: - Drinking before or during shifts - Needing a drink to unwind every single night - Defensiveness or secrecy about how much she’s drinking - Negatives showing up in her health, finances, or relationships If those signs don’t appear, odds are she’s handling it well. Encourage your parents to trust her, stay supportive, and offer to be someone she can talk to without judgment. That way, if she ever does need guidance, she’ll feel safe turning to family rather than hiding things from them.
